{"id": "20463026", "revid": "125972", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki?curid=20463026", "title": "Western Antique Aeroplane &amp; Automobile Museum", "text": "The Western Antique Aeroplane and Automobile Museum (WAAAM) is an aviation museum located at the Ken Jernstedt Airfield in Hood River, Oregon. The museum is focused on airplanes, automobiles and motorcycles from the 1900s to the 1930s.\nHistory.\nTerry Jones, the son of an FBO owner, began acquiring antique aircraft early in his life. In 2006, he founded a non-profit organization and on 7 September 2007 of the following year the Western Antique Aeroplane &amp; Automobile Museum opened with 42 aircraft and 20 cars in a building. A second hangar was added in 2009, bringing the size of the museum to , and by September of the following year the collection had grown to 75 airplanes and over 100 automobiles. A third hangar was built in 2013. By 2022, the museum had plans for two additional buildings. The museum broke ground on a fourth expansion in 2025.\nExhibits.\nThe fa\u00e7ade of a 1940s hangar used by Evergreen Flying Service was salvaged from Evergreen Airport in 2008 and placed on display.\nCollections.\nAutomobiles.\nAutomobiles include:\nEvents.\nThe museum holds an annual Hood River Fly-In on the first weekend after Labor Day. It also holds a Traffic Jam Car Show in early July.\nPrograms.\nThe museum holds driving schools for Model Ts and other vintage cars."}
